      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 0,
          "bbox": [
            0.246,
            0.092,
            0.402,
            0.11
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Think it over!"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.102,
            0.121,
            0.259,
            0.138
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Organ donation"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.1,
            0.142,
            0.548,
            0.444
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Organ donation is a generous act of donating an organ to a person who suffers from non-function of organ(s). Donation of an organ may be done by the consent of the donor and his/her family. Anyone regardless of age or gender can become an organ and tissue donor. Organ transplants can save or transform the life of a person. Transplantation is required because recipient’s organ has been damaged or has failed by disease or injury. In organ transplantation the organ is surgically removed from one person (organ donor) and transplanted to another person (the recipient). Common transplantations include corneas, kidneys, heart, liver, pancreas, lungs, intestines and bone marrow. Most organ and tissue donations occur just after the donor has died or when the doctor declares a person brain dead. But some organs such as kidney, part of a liver, lung, etc., and tissues can be donated while the donor is alive.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.591,
            0.085,
            0.853,
            0.106
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "5.5.2 Excretion in Plants"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.589,
            0.114,
            0.901,
            0.454
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Plants use completely different strategies for excretion than those of animals. Oxygen itself can be thought of as a waste product generated during photosynthesis! We have discussed earlier how plants deal with oxygen as well as",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\mathrm { C O _ { 2 ^ { \\circ } } }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "They can get rid of excess water by transpiration. For other wastes, plants use the fact that many of their tissues consist of dead cells, and that they can even lose some parts such as leaves. Many plant waste products are stored in cellular vacuoles. Waste products may be stored in leaves that fall off. Other waste products are stored as resins and gums, especially in old xylem. Plants also excrete some waste substances into the soil around them.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.258,
            0.494,
            0.744,
            0.523
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Q U E S T I O N S"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.099,
            0.535,
            0.602,
            0.553
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "1. Describe the structure and functioning of nephrons.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.555,
            0.749,
            0.573
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "2. What are the methods used by plants to get rid of excretory products?",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.574,
            0.569,
            0.593
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "3. How is the amount of urine produced regulated?",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.134,
            0.633,
            0.514,
            0.661
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "What you have learnt"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.68,
            0.711,
            0.698
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Movement of various types can be taken as an indication of life."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.7,
            0.869,
            0.733
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Maintenance of life requires processes like nutrition, respiration, transport of "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "materials within the body and excretion of waste products.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.734,
            0.869,
            0.784
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Autotrophic nutrition involves the intake of simple inorganic materials from the "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "environment and using an external energy source like the Sun to synthesise complex high-energy organic material.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.785,
            0.869,
            0.819
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Heterotrophic nutrition involves the intake of complex material prepared by other "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "organisms.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.819,
            0.869,
            0.87
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "In human beings, the food eaten is broken down by various steps along the "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "alimentary canal and the digested food is absorbed in the small intestine to be sent to all cells in the body.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "index": 15,
